.TH CHTTPSVR_REGISTER_STREAMING_HANDLER 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler \- register a streaming handler on the root router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler(chttpsvr srv,
                                  chttp_method_t method,
                                  const char *pattern,
                                  chttpsvr_handler_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Works like chttpsvr_register_handler, but marks the route as a
streaming route, so chttpsvr_req_read is available to the handler,
which reads the body one piece at a time.  chttpsvr_req_read returns
-1 on a route that is not a streaming route.

A streaming handler reads its body at the pace of the client and can
hold its thread for as long as the client takes, so it runs on a pool
of its own, of chttpsvr_config_t.streaming_thread_count threads, and
never on the reactor thread or on the worker pool that serves
buffered routes; slow streaming clients can occupy at most that pool.
The server creates the pool when it receives its first streaming
request.  The thread counts as a worker of its server: a call that a
handler must not make on its own server, such as chttpsvr_destroy()
or a restart, is refused or fatal there exactly as it is in a buffered
handler.  Setting chttpsvr_config_t.streaming_thread_count to
CHTTPSVR_STREAMING_POOL_OFF runs streaming handlers on the worker pool
instead; chttpsvr_start(3) describes what that changes.

You can use CHTTP_ANY as method to match every HTTP method on the
pattern; see chttpsvr_register_handler for the full rules of
CHTTP_ANY.

The server routes the request as soon as it has parsed the headers,
before it reads any body byte, so the worker reads the body itself,
batch by batch, directly off the socket; the reactor thread never
reads it.  chttpsvr_req_read hands each batch to the handler as it
arrives.  It returns 0 once the body has ended, and every later call
also returns 0, so the EOF is idempotent.  It returns -1 for a dropped
connection, for a body above max_body_size, and for a client that
stalled.  chttpsvr_config_t.stream_read_timeout_us bounds each gap,
and chttpsvr_config_t.max_body_read_duration_us, when set, bounds the
whole read; call chttpsvr_req_stream_error to tell these causes apart.
chttpsvr_config_t.min_transfer_rate_bps also ends, with
ccol_timed_out, the read of a client whose average rate falls below
that floor.

A streaming request that finds every streaming thread busy waits,
without a thread, in a queue of
chttpsvr_config_t.streaming_queue_capacity entries.  When that queue
is full, and when a request waits there longer than
chttpsvr_config_t.streaming_queue_timeout_us, the server answers at
once with 503 Service Unavailable and "Retry-After: 5", and the
handler never runs for such a request.  None of this blocks the
reactor thread.

The pattern rules of chttpsvr_register_handler apply here as well:
pattern must start with '/', a pattern with two consecutive slashes or
a trailing slash is rejected, a {name} segment must contain only
[A-Za-z0-9_], and the same {name} must not appear more than once in
one pattern.  See chttpsvr_register_handler for the full details of
the pattern checks and for how a path segment is decoded.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID or a stale handle that
                  has already been destroyed; when pattern or fn is
                  NULL; when pattern does not start with '/'; when
                  pattern has two consecutive slashes or a trailing
                  slash; when a {name} segment contains a character
                  outside [A-Za-z0-9_]; or when the same {name}
                  appears more than once.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_register_handler (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_read (3),
.BR CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT (3)
